WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.730
SPEAKER: Let me show you how
Google can help you secure some

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.040
of your most sensitive data.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.630
Most companies
today have a policy

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.060
around data minimization,
the goal being

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.600
to minimize the amount of data
that you collect and store

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.290
to only the data that's actually
needed to run your business.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.490
But that's easy to do in words,
but hard to do in practice.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.110
Data has a way of leaking
out all over the place.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:24.840
So let's look at a
concrete example.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.790
We have an enterprise
application

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.390
that a support agent might use
to chat with your end users.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.940
And in the chat you can see
Neil is a support agent,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.250
and he's asked Alice
for some information

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.690
to verify her account.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.580
Well, Alice has way over-shared.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.860
We just wanted the last
four of her social.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.470
She gave us her full social,
her phone number, and a picture

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.530
of her social security card.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.560
Now, obviously we want to
store this chat, right?

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.530
To see how our
conversations are going,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.880
or how the agents are working.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.969
But I can't store
that in a database.

00:00:55.969 --> 00:00:57.510
I'm going to have
half my company now

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.010
knowing Alice's social
security number.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.110
So how do we do that?

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.370
DLP makes it easy.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.340
When he ends the chat, we
will use Data Loss Prevention

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.670
to identify and redact
sensitive information.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.640
So now you'll see that we've--

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.473
[HOOT FROM AUDIENCE]

00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:13.098
Yes.

00:01:13.098 --> 00:01:17.230
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:21.160
We recognize over 40 different
types of sensitive info types.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.440
In this case we've
identified Alice's name.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.940
If he scrolls down
a little bit, you

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.520
can see we found her phone
number, her social security

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.520
number, we replaced all
those with red dots.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.280
And even we went into the
picture of the social security

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.540
card, found the social security
number there, and redacted it.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.127
Now this chat can be
used for analysis.

00:01:40.127 --> 00:01:43.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:48.790
Now just to prove this is real,
Neil has a webcam over there,

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.590
and we're going to
try one more example.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.180
I was in the green
room a while ago,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.620
and I found this credit
card sitting there,

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:54.578
and it said Urs Holzle.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.860
I'm just kidding, Neil
has a sample credit

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.710
card with a valid number,
but it's not Urs's card.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:03.555
I trust DLP.

00:02:03.555 --> 00:02:07.257
I don't trust 10,000 of you
with cell phone cameras.

00:02:07.257 --> 00:02:09.340
So he's going to put the
credit card number on it,

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.880
he's going to hit the button,
we're going to use DLP,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.830
and it will find and
redact the credit card.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:21.090
Now this isn't just dumb OCR.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.486
You'll notice it didn't
blot out the expiration

00:02:24.486 --> 00:02:25.860
date or the name,
because we told

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.840
the API that the
only information we

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.640
wanted to eliminate was
credit card numbers.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.450
So you can control
your definition

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.026
of PII, what matters to you.

00:02:36.026 --> 00:02:36.900
So there you have it.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.220
Identity [INAUDIBLE] proxy.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.710
We made it super simple to
take a corporate application

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.210
and make it available
for end users,

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.110
without the hassle of complex
network configurations.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.770
And with data loss
prevention, we

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.710
were able to help it make sure
that you minimize the amount

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.860
of data that you collect.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.318
So that's one less headache
you have in terms of compliance

00:02:53.318 --> 00:02:56.630
or regulatory or really
just running your business.

